> time. But I want that abstraction to be represented in the system, and
> not have a hard coupling to email.

I would like to agree on the shape before writing code. This one
touches the script, the tests, and most of prompt.md. Today the
coupling is in these places:

- lab-process-mail: get_up_to_date rebases the mail outbox and runs
  notmuch new, oldest_inbox_message picks the next item, and the check
  after each pass asks notmuch whether the message left the inbox.
- The WORK revset leaves out ::mail@upstream by name.
- prompt.md: steps 2, 5, 6, 7 and 8 are all email (notmuch,
  format-patch, maildir-deliver, the mail workspace).

Here is what I propose. A channel is a duplex queue with four
operations, each a small executable under
libexec/lab-process-mail/channels/<name>/:

- sync - bring the channel up to date (today: rebase the outbox onto
  mail@upstream and run notmuch new).
- next - print the id of the oldest item awaiting an answer, or
  nothing.
- pending <id> - exit 0 if the item still awaits an answer. The loop
  uses this as its success signal, so control still rests on state
  the model does not author.
- revset - print the revset of the channel's own commits, so WORK can
  leave them out without naming mail@upstream.

LAB_CHANNEL picks the channel, and defaults to mail. prompt.md splits
in two: a core that is the same for every channel (sources, settling
your work, doing the work), and prompt-<channel>.md, which says how to
read an item, post a series, reply, and verify. The script joins them
when it builds the prompt.

Note that the list then plays two roles. mail@upstream is a source of
commits, which [1] covers, and it is also the channel. These are
different things, and I would keep them separate in the code. Another
channel could still name the list as a source.

The series would be:

1. Split prompt.md into the core prompt and prompt-mail.md, with no
   change in what the agent is told.
2. Move sync, next, and pending into channels/mail, and have the loop
   call them through LAB_CHANNEL.
3. Derive the WORK exclusion from the channel's revset.

Each step keeps the tests passing, and with only one channel nothing
changes in behavior. Shall I go ahead? I would also like your view on
two things: whether "channel" is the name you want, and whether a
directory of executables suits you better than a sourced shell file
per channel. I chose executables because they keep each channel's
tools, notmuch here, out of the main script.

